<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  DEBUG ONLY. This manifest is merged into debug builds and never into a
  release one.

  WHY IT HAS TO EXIST. Android has blocked cleartext HTTP by default since API
  28, and this app targets 37 — so a debug build pointed at a self-hosted
  instance on `http://host:port` (which is what a LAN or a devbox instance
  looks like) cannot reach it at all. The failure is silent in the useful
  sense: the request fails, the launcher says "could not reach", and nothing
  anywhere mentions that the platform refused to dial rather than the server
  refusing to answer.

  WHY NOT IN THE MAIN MANIFEST. Because the default is right. A shipped Talaria
  Mobile should refuse plaintext: instances are reached over TLS, the session
  cookie rides those requests, and `Secure` is exactly the flag the api sets on
  it in production. Allowing cleartext for everyone to spare a debug build one
  attribute would be trading the product's transport security for a
  convenience during development.

  So: debug can dial plaintext, release cannot, and nobody has to remember to
  undo anything before shipping.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <application android:usesCleartextTraffic="true" />
</manifest>
